2026-06-30 15:29:17,297 - openai._base_client - DEBUG - Request options: {'method': 'post', 'url': '/chat/completions', 'timeout': 300, 'files': None, 'idempotency_key': 'stainless-python-retry-0179159e-8219-4991-8666-4476220e5ccc', 'security': {'bearer_auth': True}, 'content': None, 'json_data': {'messages': [{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You are an expert proteomics assistant. Return valid JSON only.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'You are a proteomics expert analyzing crosslinking mass spectrometry (XL-MS) datasets.\n\nYour task is to cluster raw spectral files into biologically meaningful groups.\n\nA cluster should represent a unique combination of:\n1. Biological / experimental condition\n   - Examples: treatment, control, carbon source, growth medium, cell state, tissue, disease state, time point, perturbation, purified complex, recombinant standard set.\n2. Source organism of the proteome being analyzed\n   - Return the full scientific name.\n   - Use the organism of the biological sample or proteome of interest, not necessarily the expression host.\n   - Example: if human proteins are expressed in E. coli and analyzed as human recombinant standards, the source organism is Homo sapiens.\n   - If the dataset intentionally contains multiple source organisms, create separate clusters by organism when possible.\n3. Crosslinking reagent\n   - Examples: BS3, DSS, DSSO, DSBU, formaldehyde.\n   - If multiple crosslinkers are used, create separate clusters by reagent when possible.\n\nDo NOT split clusters by:\n- fraction number\n- LC fraction\n- technical replicate\n- biological replicate ID\n- injection number\n- file numbering\n- enrichment fraction\n- instrument run order\n- search batch\n- calibration/QC files\n\nReplicates, fractions, and repeated injections from the same biological condition should be grouped together.\n\n---\n\nINPUTS\n\nRaw filenames:\nQE1021122_Gordon_01.raw\nQE1021122_Gordon_04.raw\nQE1120921_Gordon_01_control.raw\nQE1120921_Gordon_03_crosslink.raw\n\nPublication text and PRIDE metadata:\n[TITLE]\nCryo-EM reveals the architecture of the PELP1-WDR18 molecular scaffold\n\n[ABSTRACT]\nPELP1 (Proline-, Glutamic acid-, Leucine-rich protein 1) is a large scaffolding protein that functions in many cellular pathways including steroid receptor (SR) coactivation, heterochromatin maintenance, and ribosome biogenesis. PELP1 is a proto-oncogene whose expression is upregulated in many human cancers, but how the PELP1 scaffold coordinates its diverse cellular functions is poorly understood. Here we show that PELP1 serves as the central scaffold for the human Rix1 complex whose members include WDR18, TEX10, and SENP3. We reconstitute the mammalian Rix1 complex and identified a stable sub-complex comprised of the conserved PELP1 Rix1 domain and WDR18. We determine a 2.7\u2009Å cryo-EM structure of the subcomplex revealing an interconnected tetrameric assembly and the architecture of PELP1’s signaling motifs, including eleven LxxLL motifs previously implicated in SR signaling and coactivation of Estrogen Receptor alpha (ERα) mediated transcription. However, the structure shows that none of these motifs is in a conformation that would support SR binding. Together this work establishes that PELP1 scaffolds the Rix1 complex, and association with WDR18 may direct PELP1’s activity away from SR coactivation.\nPELP1 is a large scaffolding protein implicated in many cellular activities, including ribosome assembly as part of the Rix1 complex, comprising PELP1, WDR18, TEX10 and other components. Here, authors present the cryo-EM structure of PELP1 in complex with its binding partner WDR18, revealing the architecture of PELP1\'s numerous signaling motifs.\n\n[METHODS]\nMethods\nCell culture\nHEK293FT cells (Thermo) were cultured in FreeStyle 293 Expression Media without supplementation at 37\u2009°C, 8% CO2, 80% humidity, 130\u2009rpm, and were used for transient human protein expression experiments/purifications. HepG2 (ATCC) cells were maintained in Opti-MEM supplemented with 10% FBS, 1% Anti-Anti, 1% GlutaMax, and were used for dual luciferase assays. MCF-7 cells were maintained in MEM supplemented with 10% FBS, 1% Penicillin/Streptomycin, 1% Glutamine, and were used for endogenous co-immunoprecipitation experiments. Special culture conditions for E2 treatments are outlined in relevant method sections below.\nRecombinant reconstitution, isolation, and detection of human Rix1 complexes\n40\u2009mL suspension cultures of HEK293FT cells were transiently transfected with plasmid vector DNA (Supplementary Table\xa01) using 293fectin reagent (Thermo) and using the manufacture’s protocol (1:2 DNA to transfection reagent ratio). 1 μg of total DNA was used per 1\u2009mL of cell culture. Amounts of DNA for individual plasmids co-transfected were equal. Cells were incubated for at least 48\u2009hours to allow for protein expression and were then harvested by centrifugation as 20\u2009mL cell pellets. Cell pellets were stored at −80\u2009°C until use. Each cell pellet was lysed in 1\u2009mL lysis buffer (50\u2009mM Hepes pH 7.3, 200\u2009mM NaCl, 5\u2009mM Mg2Cl, 10% Glycerol, 0.5% NP-40, EDTA-free protease inhibitor (Roche), 1.75 ×10−4\u2009U/mL Benzonase (Sigma)) for 30\u2009min at 4\u2009°C with gentle agitation on a nutator. Whole cell lysate was then clarified by centrifugation at 17k× g for 35\u2009minutes at 4\u2009°C. The anti-FLAG M2 Affinity Gel (Sigma) was used for affinity isolation of FLAG-tagged Rix1 complex members and reconstituted complexes. Approximately 20 μL of equilibrated anti-FLAG gel was incubated with 1\u2009mL of clarified input lysate per sample for 1\u2009h (4\u2009°C on a nutator). Anti-FLAG gel with bound protein was then washed with 2\u2009mL of lysis buffer, 0.2\u2009mL of ATP wash buffer (50\u2009mM Hepes pH 7.3, 150\u2009mM NaCl, 20\u2009mM Mg2Cl, 5% Glycerol, 5\u2009mM ATP) 3 times with each for 5-minute incubations on ice, and finally 2\u2009mL high salt wash buffer (50\u2009mM Hepes pH 7.5, 600\u2009mM NaCl, 5\u2009mM Mg2Cl, 5% Glycerol). 5 μL of washed and protein-bound anti-FLAG gel was used for SDS-PAGE and Western blot. Remaining 15 μL was transferred to a SigmaPrep spin column (Sigma) and used for native elution of protein using 3X FLAG peptide. 15 µL of 300 μg/mL 3X FLAG peptide (Pierce) in high salt wash buffer was incubated with 15 µL of protein-bound anti-FLAG gel for 30\u2009minutes at 4\u2009°C and on a nutator. Eluted protein complexes were collected by centrifugation and subject to SDS-PAGE and total protein staining (SimplySafe Stain). For Western Blot of recombinant proteins input lysate and complexes bound to anti-FLAG gel, samples were boiled at 95\u2009°C for 10\u2009min, loaded on an 4–15% Polyacrylamide gel, and ran at 200\u2009V for 30\u2009min before separated proteins were transferred onto a nitrocellulose membrane (BioRad). Membranes were blocked for 1.5\u2009h at room temperature in 5% milk in TBST (0.1% TWEEN-20) and then incubated with primary antibody overnight at 4\u2009°C. Primary antibody mixtures were either anti-FLAG (Sigma #7425), anti-HA (Invitrogen #26183), anti-GFP (Roche #11814460001), anti-MYC (Sigma #05-724), or anti-Tubulin (Invitrogen #MA1-80017) in 5% milk, 1% BSA in TBST. The following morning, membranes were washed three times with TBST and incubated for 1\u2009h at room temperature with secondary antibody (anti-mouse HRP or anti-rabbit HRP, Sigma) in 5% milk, 1% BSA in TBST. Membranes were then washed three more times in TBST and then exposed using a BioRad chemiluminescent imager.\nBS3 crosslinking mass spectrometry\nPurified complex (6.0 μM for PELP1 Rix1 domain – WDR18 sub-complex and 2.0 μM for Rix1 complex) was crosslinked with 1.0\u2009mM and 1.2\u2009mM, respectively, of bis(sulfosuccinimidyl)suberate (BS3; Sigma) in (50\u2009mM Hepes pH 7.3, 600\u2009mM NaCl, 5\u2009mM MgCl2, 5% Glycerol) at room temperature for 10\u2009min before quenching with 300\u2009mM Tris pH 7.5 for 15\u2009min at 4\u2009°C. 5 μL of the crosslinked complex was diluted to 20 μL and digested with addition of 1 μL trypsin (0.1 μg/μL – Promega) in 50\u2009mM ammonium bicarbonate buffer overnight at 37\u2009°C. The digests were then stored at −80\u2009°C for subsequent MS analysis. Protein digests were analyzed by LC/MS on a Q Exactive Plus mass spectrometer (ThermoFisher Scientific) interfaced with an M-Class nanoAcquity UPLC system (Waters Corporation) equipped with a 75 μm x 150\u2009mm HSS T3 C18 column (1.8 μm particle, Waters Corporation) and a C18 trapping column (180 μm × 20\u2009mm) with 5 μm particle size at a flow rate of 450 nL/min. The trapping column was in-line with the analytical column and upstream of a micro-tee union which was used for venting, trapping, and as a liquid junction. Trapping was performed using the initial solvent composition. 5 μL of digested sample was injected onto the column. Peptides were eluted by using a linear gradient from 99% solvent A (0.1% formic acid in water (v/v)) and 1% solvent B (0.1% formic acid in acetonitrile (v/v)) to 40% solvent B over 70\u2009minutes. For the mass spectrometry, a top-ten data-dependent acquisition method was employed with a dynamic exclusion time of 15\u2009seconds and exclusion of singly charged species. The mass spectrometer was employed with a nanoflex source with a stainless-steel needle and used in the positive ion mode. Instrument parameters were as follows: sheath gas, 0; auxiliary gas, 0; sweep gas, 0; spray voltage, 2.7\u2009kV; capillary temperature, 275\u2009°C; S-lens, 60; scan range (m/z) of 375 to 1500; 1.6\u2009m/z isolation window; resolution: 70,000 (MS), 17,500 (MS/MS); automated gain control (AGC), 3 ×106\u2009ions (MS), 5 ×104 (MS/MS); and a maximum IT of 100\u2009ms (MS), 50\u2009ms (MS/MS). Mass calibration was performed before data acquisition using the Pierce LTQ Velos Positive Ion Calibration mixture (ThermoFisher Scientific). The LC/MS raw data were first converted to an MGF format using Mascot Distiller from Matrix Science and then analyzed using the Batch-Tag Web function of the Protein Prospector web-based software developed by the UCSF Mass Spectrometry Facility. The MGF file was searched against sequences for members of the human Rix1 complex by employing the User Protein Sequence field with other search parameters, including tryptic specificity and 3 missed cleavages; precursor charge range of 2, 3, 4, and 5; monoisotopic values; parent mass tolerance of 20 ppm and fragment mass tolerance of 50 ppm; oxidation of methionine and incorrect monoisotopic assignment as a variable modifications; and in the Crosslinking field, the Link Search Type was defined as DSS. The putative crosslinked peptide output was triaged by limiting the mass error of putative crosslinks to two standard deviations from the average error (about 5 ppm); requiring a Score Difference value >4 except for the cases of intermolecular crosslinks of identical peptides or peptides less than or equal to 3 amino acid residues; and total expectation values below 1 ×10−4.\nPurification of PELP1-WDR18 and cryo-EM sample preparation\nN-terminally FLAG-tagged PELP1 Rix1 domain (1-642aa) and C-terminally HA-tagged WDR18 protein was transiently co-expressed in 100\u2009mL of HEK293FT cells using same expression protocol as above. 100\u2009mL cell pellet was lysed using a high salt lysis buffer (600\u2009mM NaCl, 50\u2009mM Hepes pH 7.7, 5\u2009mM MgCl2, 5% Glycerol, 0.5% NP-40, EDTA-free protease inhibitor, 1.75 ×10−4\u2009U/mL Benzonase) for 30\u2009minutes at 4\u2009°C with gentle agitation on a nutator. Whole cell lysate was then clarified by centrifugation at 17k× g for 40\u2009minutes at 4\u2009°C. The anti-FLAG M2 Affinity Gel (Sigma) was used for affinity purification of FLAG-tagged PELP1 Rix1 domain bound to WDR18 by incubating clarified lysate with anti-FLAG gel for 1.5\u2009h at 4\u2009°C with gentle agitation on a nutator. Anti-FLAG gel with bound protein was washed 2 times with 10\u2009mL high salt lysis buffer and 2 more times with 10\u2009mL low salt buffer (150\u2009mM NaCl, 50\u2009mM Hepes pH 7.7, 5\u2009mM MgCl2). Bound protein was eluted from the anti-FLAG resin using 300 μg/mL 3X FLAG peptide in low salt buffer using two separate elutions. Each elution was incubated with anti-FLAG gel in a SigmaPrep spin column for 45\u2009minutes at 4\u2009°C with gentle rocking before the collection of eluted protein by ce\n\nPreviously extracted organism(s) and NCBI taxid(s):\nHomo sapiens (human)\n\n---\n\nTASKS\n\n1. Identify the distinct biological/experimental conditions described by the publication, PRIDE metadata, and filenames.\n\n2. Assign every raw file to exactly one cluster.\n\n3. Define clusters using the smallest number of biologically meaningful groups. Do not over-cluster based only on replicate, fraction, or run-number differences.\n\n4. For each cluster, report:\n   - cluster_id: "cluster_1", "cluster_2", etc.\n   - cluster_label: short machine-readable label using lowercase words separated by underscores\n   - description: concise human-readable summary of the condition\n   - organism: full scientific name of the proteome/sample organism\n   - taxid: NCBI Taxonomy CURIE, e.g. "NCBITaxon:9606"; use null if unknown\n   - crosslinker: crosslinking reagent; use null if unknown\n   - biological_condition: short phrase describing the treatment/control/state\n   - files: list of raw filenames assigned to this cluster\n   - confidence: "high", "medium", or "low"\n   - evidence_quote: exact sentence or short phrase from the publication, PRIDE metadata, or filename pattern that supports this cluster assignment\n   - reasoning: brief explanation of why these files belong together\n\n5. Assign files that cannot be confidently interpreted to cluster_0 with:\n   - cluster_label: "unassigned"\n   - confidence: "low"\n   - evidence_quote: null\n   - reasoning: explain why assignment was not possible\n\n6. QC, calibrant, blank, wash, or test files should be assigned to cluster_0 unless the publication clearly states they are part of the biological experiment.\n\n7. If all biological files appear to come from one condition, create only cluster_1 plus cluster_0 if there are unassigned/QC files.\n\n8. Confidence guidelines:\n   - "high": filenames and publication/metadata agree clearly\n   - "medium": assignment is likely but partially inferred from filenames or incomplete metadata\n   - "low": weak evidence, ambiguous naming, missing metadata, or conflicting information\n\n9. If publication text and filenames conflict, prioritize the publication/PRIDE metadata, but mention the conflict in reasoning.\n\n10. Return ONLY valid JSON. Do not include markdown, comments, or explanatory text outside the JSON.\n\n---\n\nOUTPUT JSON STRUCTURE\n\n{\n  "clusters": [\n    {\n      "cluster_id": "cluster_1",\n      "cluster_label": "yeast_glucose_bs3",\n      "description": "Saccharomyces cerevisiae samples grown in glucose-rich medium and crosslinked with BS3.",\n      "organism": "Saccharomyces cerevisiae",\n      "taxid": "NCBITaxon:4932",\n      "crosslinker": "BS3",\n      "biological_condition": "glucose-rich medium",\n      "files": [\n        "YeastGlu_R1.raw",\n        "YeastGlu_R2.raw",\n        "YeastGlu_fraction_01.raw"\n      ],\n      "confidence": "high",\n      "evidence_quote": "Yeast cells were cultured in glucose- or glycerol-containing medium and crosslinked with BS3.",\n      "reasoning": "The filenames contain Glu and the publication states that glucose-grown yeast samples were crosslinked with BS3."\n    },\n    {\n      "cluster_id": "cluster_0",\n      "cluster_label": "unassigned",\n      "description": "Files that could not be confidently assigned to a biological XL-MS condition.",\n      "organism": null,\n      "taxid": null,\n      "crosslinker": null,\n      "biological_condition": null,\n      "files": [\n        "calibrant.raw",\n        "QC.raw"\n      ],\n      "confidence": "low",\n      "evidence_quote": null,\n      "reasoning": "These files appear to be QC or calibration runs rather than biological samples."\n    }\n  ],\n  "summary": {\n    "n_input_files": 5,\n    "n_assigned_files": 3,\n    "n_unassigned_files": 2,\n    "n_biological_clusters": 1,\n    "overall_confidence": "high"\n  }\n}'}], 'model': 'gpt-5.4-nano-2026-03-17', 'store': True, 'temperature': 0.3}}
